          "answer": "**Please call emergency services now (for example 911 or 112) or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if it passes.**\n\nA headache that comes on suddenly, like a bang, and reaches full strength within about a minute is a \"thunderclap\" headache. The emergency medicine guidance (Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns) lists this as a reason to call emergency services immediately, and describes it as often \"the worst headache of one's life.\" Your description matches that pattern. The triage tool did not flag it, but that tool only checks a fixed list and cannot clear your symptoms, so please do not rely on its \"routine\" rating.\n\nDo not drive yourself. Tell someone near you what happened, and bring a list of anything you have taken. The same guidance also lists sudden severe eye pain or blurred vision as urgent. Your sensitivity to light is not on that list, but it is worth telling the medical team.\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis. Only a clinician who examines you can tell what is causing this.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
